# Chroma library, modern Earth: ADULT stage (ages about 25 to 45). Grid: earth-plan.md. Rules: library-spec.md.
# Syntax: header of build.py. Compile and check the whole batch: python3 build.py earth

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 1: calls WU (cycle s4) / calls BRG (cycle s2)

== a performance review
tier: everyday
at: employer
stages: adult
age: 24-60
calls: WU
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague
earth: the yearly review with your manager, and a form that asks you to rate yourself first
times: Most employed adults, once a year; formal yearly reviews are the norm in offices and larger firms, less so in small or manual work (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: {N} has a meeting with {boss} on Thursday, and a form that asks {N} to score the year from one to five.
scene W: The review form has twelve boxes, and {N} means to fill in every one honestly, the way the company asks.
scene U: {N} reads the review criteria twice, looking for what is actually measured and what is only said.
scene B: Raises are decided in these meetings, and {colleague} has already booked a long lunch with {boss}.
scene R: An hour of sitting across from {boss} talking about strengths and weaknesses. {N} already wants to scream.
scene G: It is the fourth review at the same desk. Outside the meeting room the leaves are turning, and {N} wonders how much of a year fits on a form.
- W | fill in the self-assessment honestly, weak spots included | v: conformity | mark: owned up | chance: 60
- U | bring a page of figures showing exactly what your work achieved | v: achievement | chance: 75
- B | name the raise you want before your boss can name a lower one | v: power, achievement | chance: 69
- R | tell your boss plainly what is wrong with how the team is run | v: self-direction | mark: defied an authority | chance: 58
- G | hear the feedback out without arguing, and keep to the work you are good at | v: tradition (humility) | chance: 90
- W>G | put in a formal request to work four days a week and be home more | v: benevolence | chance: 50
- U>W | propose clear, fair criteria for reviewing the whole team | v: universalism | identity | chance: 45
- B>U | flatter your boss into giving you the project you would learn most from | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 71
- R>B | threaten, in the heat of the meeting, to walk out unless they promote you | v: power | chance: 30
- G>R | take the middling score in your stride and keep your evenings for what you love | v: hedonism | chance: 82
works: {boss} nods along, and the form goes upstairs with a good word written on it.
works: The meeting ends early, with a handshake and a better number than {N} expected.
fails: The meeting runs long, and the phrase "area for development" comes up four times.
fails: The rating lands a point lower than last year, and nobody can say quite why.

== a neighbour's dog keeps you awake
tier: everyday
stages: adult
age: 24-60
calls: BRG
tone: trouble
life: home, conflict
horizon: week
roles: a neighbour, partner
earth: the dog next door barks half the night, every night
times: About 1 in 3 adults, once or twice in a life, usually after a move; barking dogs are a common cause of neighbour noise complaints (estimate)
gap: 6-12
share: .33
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: For the third night running the dog next door barks from midnight until two, and {N} lies staring at the ceiling.
scene W: There are rules about noise after eleven, and the dog next door breaks them every night. The council's number is on the fridge.
scene U: The barking starts at ten past twelve and stops at ten to two, almost to the minute. {N} begins to wonder what sets it off.
scene B: {N} has an early meeting, and the dog next door has other plans. Somebody is going to pay for this lost sleep.
scene R: At two in the morning the dog is still at it, and {N} is out of bed in the dark with clenched fists.
scene G: The dog next door is left alone in the yard all night, howling like its wild cousins. {N} hears loneliness in it.
- W | log the times and report the noise to the council | v: conformity, security | chance: 40
- U | try earplugs, a fan and heavy curtains until something works | v: self-direction | chance: 85
- B | hint to the neighbours that the landlord might hear about their other habits | v: power | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: a threat; backfire: a feud over the fence | chance: 62
- R | bang on their door at two in the morning and have it out | v: none clear (anger let out) | body: light | closed: approval: a scene in the street; backfire: the police are called | chance: 48
- G | get to know the neighbours properly, and sort it out between you as friends | door | v: benevolence, security | chance: 60
- W>B | quote the tenancy rules to their landlord until the dog has to go | v: power, security | closed: approval: getting a family's dog taken away; backfire: the street turns against you | chance: 20
- U>R | record the barking and turn it into a ridiculous song for the street's group chat | v: stimulation | closed: approval: mocking the neighbours in front of the whole street; backfire: the neighbours take offence, and the feud is on | tech: phone | chance: 40
- B>G | pay the neighbours' teenager to walk the dog every evening so it sleeps | v: universalism (nature), security | chance: 79
- R>W | march round in the morning and make them promise the dog sleeps indoors | v: security, conformity | chance: 75
- G>U | sit out in the yard at night, listening, until you understand what frightens it | v: self-direction, universalism (nature) | chance: 35
works: The barking stops, or {N} stops hearing it, and sleep comes back.
works: By the end of the week the street is quiet after midnight.
fails: The dog barks on, and now the neighbours no longer say hello.
fails: A complaint arrives in the post, about the noise {N} made.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 2: calls UB (cycle s1) / calls WRG (cycle s3)

== a rival at work
tier: everyday
at: employer
stages: adult
age: 24-60
calls: UB
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: months
roles: rival, boss, colleague
earth: a colleague who started the same month keeps getting the projects you wanted
times: Most working adults, every few years, with a new job or a new team (estimate)
gap: 3-8
scene: {rival} started the same month as {N}, and has somehow ended up with every project {N} wanted.
scene W: {rival} cuts corners and takes the credit, and {N} has watched the rules bend for them all year.
scene U: {N} has been studying {rival} for months: the emails sent at seven in the morning, the way they speak in meetings.
scene B: There is one promotion this spring, and only {N} and {rival} are in the running.
scene R: {rival} smirks across the meeting table, and {N} feels the heat rise to their face.
scene G: Two people with the same gifts in the same small team, {N} thinks; one of them was always going to have to grow somewhere else.
- W | report your rival anonymously for every rule they bend | v: conformity | closed: approval: an anonymous report; backfire: traced back to you, and trust lost | chance: 35
- U | study what your rival does well, and learn to do it better | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- B | ask for the hardest project nobody wants, and make it a win | v: achievement, power | chance: 50
- R | tell your rival to their face that you see exactly what they are doing | v: none clear (honesty of feeling) | chance: 58
- G | ask to move to a team where your gifts fit better | v: security, self-direction | door | chance: 65
- W>U | ask for a formal mentor, so you grow on merit and not on politics | door | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- U>B | read your rival's emails whenever they leave their screen unlocked | habit | v: power | closed: approval: snooping on a colleague's account; backfire: caught, and a disciplinary hearing | tech: computer | chance: 45
- B>R | line up a better offer elsewhere and leave with a flourish | v: self-direction, stimulation | door | chance: 68
- R>G | challenge your rival to a game of squash and let it settle there | v: stimulation, benevolence | body: light | chance: 68
- G>W | keep doing your share without complaint, so the team holds together | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 55
works: By spring {N} is the one people come to, and {rival} knows it.
works: The tension eases, and the work starts going to whoever does it best.
fails: {rival} gets the promotion, and the corridor feels longer every morning.
fails: Word gets around, and {N} is the one who looks petty.

== a child's birthday party in the family
tier: everyday
stages: adult
age: 24-55
calls: WRG
tone: joy
life: family
horizon: week
roles: partner, parent, sibling, the birthday child, other parents
earth: the youngest in the family turns six, your own child or a niece or nephew, and the party is at your house
times: Most parents, once a year while their children are small, and a niece's or nephew's now and then (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: The youngest in the family turns six on Saturday, the party is at {Ns} house, and twelve small guests are coming.
scene W: {N} has the list: twelve invitations and twelve party bags, so that no child in the class is left out.
scene U: {N} has spent a week designing a treasure hunt whose clues get harder as the afternoon goes on.
scene B: Last month {rival} hired a magician for a party just like this one. {N} has no intention of being outdone.
scene R: Balloons on the ceiling, music far too loud, and the birthday child running in circles, shrieking with joy.
scene G: {N} bakes the same lemon cake {parent} baked for every birthday, and the kitchen smells of childhood.
- W | invite the whole class so that no child is left out | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 75
- U | design a treasure hunt with clues for every age | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 82
- B | hire the most impressive entertainer in town, and let the other parents see it | identity | v: power (face) | chance: 90
- R | turn the music up loud and dance with the children till they drop | v: hedonism, stimulation | body: light | chance: 92
- G | bake the cake that was baked for every birthday of your own childhood | v: tradition | habit | chance: 88
- W>R | draw up a timetable of games so the fun never stops | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 78
- U>G | hand out magnifying glasses and send the children on a bug hunt outside | v: universalism (nature) | chance: 75
- B>W | put the pushiest parent in charge of the games, so they cannot criticise | v: security | chance: 78
- R>U | let the children build a fizzing volcano in the garden and see what happens | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 88
- G>B | invite only the families worth knowing, as your own parents always did | v: power (face), tradition | closed: approval: snubbing the rest of the class; backfire: the excluded families talk | chance: 40
works: The cake arrives whole, and the birthday child falls asleep on the sofa with icing on one cheek.
works: The children leave happy, and two parents ask for the recipe.
fails: Two guests cry, one is sick, and the cake slides off the plate.
fails: Half the class does not come, and the birthday child notices.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 3: calls BR (cycle s2) / calls WUG (cycle s4)

== a big night out after years of routine
tier: everyday
stages: adult
age: 26-55
calls: BR
tone: joy
life: leisure, friends
horizon: moment
roles: friend, partner, old friends
earth: an old friend's fortieth in a club in town, the first time the old crowd has met in years
times: Most adults, every few years, at a friend's round birthday, a wedding or a reunion (estimate)
gap: 3-6
scene: {friend} is turning forty, the old crowd is meeting for the first time in years, and the babysitter is booked until two.
scene W: {N} promised {partner} to be home by two, and to keep an eye on {friend}, who never knows when to stop.
scene U: {N} has not been out dancing in six years, and wonders with real curiosity what has changed out there.
scene B: Half the guests are people {N} has wanted to meet for years, and the drinks are on {friend}.
scene R: The music hits {N} the moment the door opens, and six years of early nights fall away.
scene G: The old crowd is all here, greyer and softer, laughing the same laughs as twenty years ago.
- W | stay sober so everyone gets home safe, as you promised | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 92
- U | watch the crowd and work out how a night out has changed | v: self-direction | chance: 82
- B | spend the night working the room and collecting useful numbers | habit | door | v: power, achievement | chance: 85
- R | dance until the lights come on and forget what time it is | v: hedonism | body: light | habit | chance: 92
- G | step outside with your oldest friend and talk under the night sky | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: made a friend | chance: 90
- W>B | give the birthday toast yourself, and make sure the room remembers who gave it | v: power (face) | chance: 75
- U>R | plan the perfect route: three bars, a band, and a late-night food stall | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 55
- B>G | slip the bouncer some cash so the whole old crowd gets in together | v: benevolence | chance: 82
- R>W | make a sudden, heartfelt speech that brings the old crowd back together | v: benevolence | identity | chance: 78
- G>U | sit at the bar and hear out a stranger's whole life story | v: universalism, self-direction | door | chance: 78
works: {N} gets home at dawn, hoarse and happy, with a story for every year of routine.
works: The old crowd swear to do it again next month, and this time they mean it.
fails: The morning after is brutal, and so is the bank statement.
fails: Something said at two in the morning is still being repeated a week later.

== the house needs repairs
tier: everyday
stages: adult
age: 24-60
calls: WUG
tone: trouble
life: home
horizon: months
roles: partner, a roofer, a neighbour, parent
earth: a damp patch on the ceiling, and the roofer says it is worse than it looks
times: Most homeowners, a big unexpected repair every few years; about 2 in 3 US homes are lived in by their owners (US Census); the repair spacing is an estimate
gap: 2-5
scene: A damp patch is spreading across the bedroom ceiling, and the roofer says it is worse than it looks.
scene W: The roof is {Ns} responsibility now, and so is everyone who sleeps under it.
scene U: {N} climbs into the loft with a torch to trace exactly where the water is getting in.
scene B: The roofer's quote is three times what {N} expected, and {N} suspects someone is being taken for a ride.
scene R: Water drips into a bucket by the bed all night, tap, tap, tap, and {N} has had enough.
scene G: The house is a hundred years old, and every winter it lets in a little more of the weather, the way old houses do.
- W | get three proper quotes and hire a certified roofer | v: security, conformity | chance: 75
- U | learn to fix the roof from manuals and videos, and do it properly | v: self-direction | mark: learned a skill | body: heavy | chance: 45
- B | haggle the roofer down hard, with a real rival quote in your hand | v: power (resources) | chance: 65
- R | climb up in the rain tonight and patch it yourself | v: stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | body: heavy | chance: 38
- G | call the old builder your family has always used, and do what he says | v: tradition | closed: law: the old builder is not certified; backfire: the insurer will not pay | chance: 65
- W>G | put a little aside every month to look after the old house | v: security, tradition | habit | chance: 55
- U>W | draw up a repair plan for the whole house, room by room, with a budget | v: security | chance: 75
- B>U | offer the roofer cash in hand if he teaches you how it is done | v: self-direction | mark: learned a skill | act: offer the roofer cash in hand if he teaches them how it is done | chance: 75
- R>B | rip out the old ceiling in one furious weekend and save thousands | v: power (resources) | body: heavy | chance: 35
- G>R | let the old house be imperfect, and spend the money on a summer by the sea | v: hedonism | chance: 20
works: The ceiling dries, and the house feels sound again.
works: The job comes in under budget, and the bedroom stays dry through the storms.
fails: The leak moves two feet to the left and keeps dripping.
fails: The repair costs twice the quote, and the damp is back by spring.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 4: calls RG (cycle s3) / calls WUB (cycle s1)

== a weekend with nothing planned
tier: everyday
stages: adult
age: 24-60
calls: RG
tone: joy
life: leisure
horizon: moment
roles: partner, child, friend
earth: the calendar is empty from Friday night to Monday morning, for the first time in months
times: Most adults, a few times a year; parents of young children far less often (estimate)
gap: 0-0.5
scene: For the first time in months the calendar is empty from Friday night to Monday morning.
scene W: An empty weekend, and the list of jobs {N} promised to get round to is a long one.
scene U: Two free days. {N} has a stack of half-read books and a question that has nagged for weeks.
scene B: Two whole days when nobody needs anything from {N}. That time is worth something, and {N} means to spend it well.
scene R: Friday night, nothing planned, a full tank of petrol, and the road out of town is calling.
scene G: Saturday comes up slow and bright, the garden wet with dew, and nobody needs {N} anywhere.
- W | work through the jobs you promised to do weeks ago | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: kept your word | chance: 80
- U | spend the weekend getting to the bottom of something you have always wondered about | v: self-direction | door | chance: 82
- B | use the quiet days to get ahead on work nobody else is doing | v: achievement | habit | chance: 88
- R | head out of town with no destination and see where you end up | v: stimulation | door | chance: 90
- G | sleep late, potter in the garden and let the days unfold | v: hedonism | chance: 93
- W>R | sign up for the town's fun run you always meant to try | v: stimulation | body: light | chance: 85
- U>G | look up the old footpaths and find the spring your grandparents used to talk about | v: universalism (nature), tradition | chance: 45
- B>W | sell the clutter in the garage and give the takings to the school | v: benevolence | chance: 78
- R>U | take apart something broken on a whim, just to see if you can fix it | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 75
- G>B | pick the apples in the abandoned orchard down the lane and keep them | v: power (resources) | closed: law: not your orchard; backfire: the owner turns up | chance: 85
works: By Sunday evening {N} feels rested in a way no holiday quite manages.
works: The weekend turns up a small adventure that gets retold for years.
fails: The weekend slips away in small chores and scrolling, and Monday comes too soon.
fails: The plan goes wrong on Saturday morning, and the rest of the weekend goes with it.

== a budget that will not balance
tier: everyday
stages: adult
age: 24-60
calls: WUB
tone: trouble
life: money
horizon: months
roles: partner, parent, the bank
earth: the mortgage went up, the childcare went up, and every month ends below zero
times: Many adults, a tight stretch every few years; about 1 in 3 US adults could not cover a $400 surprise bill with cash (Federal Reserve, 2023)
gap: 2-5
scene: The mortgage has gone up, the childcare has gone up, and every month the account dips below zero before payday.
scene W: {N} has never missed a bill in their life, and does not mean to start now.
scene U: {N} opens a spreadsheet and starts sorting a year of bank statements into columns.
scene B: Somewhere in this mess money is leaking away, and {N} means to find it and keep it.
scene R: Another brown envelope on the doormat. {N} wants to tear it in half and go out for dinner anyway.
scene G: {Ns} grandparents raised five children on one wage and a vegetable patch. {N} keeps thinking about that.
- W | cut every treat until the bills are paid, and stick to it | v: security, conformity | habit | chance: 60
- U | track every purchase for three months and find the leaks | v: security, self-direction | habit | chance: 62
- B | move the debt to a new card with a teaser rate and keep spending | v: power (resources) | binds | chance: 40
- R | take on weekend gigs doing something you love, until the gap is closed | habit | v: hedonism, security | self_control: + | chance: 60
- G | dig a vegetable patch and cook from what it gives | v: security, tradition | habit | chance: 22
- W>U | sign up for the bank's free money course and do every exercise | v: self-direction, conformity | mark: learned a skill | chance: 50
- U>B | find every tax credit and allowance the rules permit, and claim them all | v: power (resources), security | chance: 65
- B>R | borrow from your parents again, and book the holiday anyway | v: hedonism | binds | chance: 45
- R>G | sell the car on impulse and walk or cycle everywhere from now on | habit | v: universalism (nature), security | chance: 75
- G>W | ask the family to pool money, the way your grandparents' generation did | v: tradition, security | binds | chance: 40
works: By the third month the account stays above zero.
works: The numbers finally line up, and {N} sleeps through the night.
fails: An unexpected bill arrives and undoes a month of work.
fails: The overdraft grows, and so do the arguments about it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 5: calls WG (cycle s4) / calls UBR (cycle s2); both need a partner

== a holiday dinner with both families
tier: everyday
stages: adult
age: 24-60
calls: WG
tone: mixed
life: family, tradition
horizon: moment
roles: partner, parent, in-laws, child
needs: partner
domain: partner1
earth: the holidays, and for the first time your family and your partner's at one table
times: Many couples, every few years at the holidays, as the families take turns to host (estimate)
gap: 2-4
scene: {N} and {partner} are hosting both families for the holiday dinner: fourteen people around a table built for eight.
scene W: There is a right order to these things: who carves, who says grace, who sits at the head. {N} has thought it all through.
scene U: {N} has drawn the seating plan four times, working out who must never sit next to whom.
scene B: {partner}'s father has never thought {N} quite good enough. Today, with everyone watching, that can change.
scene R: By the second glass, an uncle is already circling the old argument about politics, and {N} feels the evening start to tip.
scene G: Two families, two sets of customs: one side lights candles, the other sings. The children want both.
- W | keep every custom of both families, in the proper order | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 62
- U | seat everyone so the old feuds never share a corner of the table | v: security | chance: 72
- B | spend the whole evening winning over your partner's father | v: power (face), achievement | chance: 55
- R | start the old songs after pudding, and get both families singing | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 60
- G | cook your grandmother's dishes and let the food bring everyone together | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 72
- W>G | start a new tradition: both families, the same table, every year | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 58
- U>W | brief your partner beforehand on every subject to steer away from | v: security, conformity | chance: 75
- B>U | keep the old aunts' glasses full and learn the family secrets nobody mentions | v: self-direction | chance: 68
- R>B | tell the whole table, loudly, whose house this is | v: power | chance: 15
- G>R | march both families out after the meal for a walk and a game in the park | v: hedonism, benevolence | body: light | chance: 65
works: The two families leave as one noisy crowd, already arguing about who hosts next year.
works: Someone from the other side asks for a recipe, and something thaws.
fails: The old argument breaks out over dessert, and two people leave early.
fails: {partner} and {N} do the washing-up in a bitter silence.

== someone at work flirts with you
tier: everyday
at: employer
stages: adult
age: 24-60
calls: UBR
tone: mixed
life: love, work
horizon: months
roles: colleague, partner
needs: partner
domain: partner1
earth: a colleague lingers at your desk, laughs at your jokes and suggests a drink after work
times: Most adults, every few years in a working life; about 1 in 3 US workers say they have had a workplace romance (SHRM 2023)
gap: 3-8
scene: {colleague} has started lingering at {Ns} desk, laughing a little too long at {Ns} jokes, and has suggested a drink after work.
scene W: Everyone in the office knows about {partner}. Still, {colleague} keeps finding reasons to stop by.
scene U: {N} notices every small signal {colleague} sends, and cannot stop turning them over like a puzzle.
scene B: {colleague} sits close to the head of the department, and the attention is flattering in more ways than one.
scene R: Something jumps in {Ns} chest every time {colleague} walks in. It has been years since {N} felt that.
scene G: {N} goes home to {partner}, to the warm kitchen and the old familiar arguments, and thinks about {colleague} all evening anyway.
- W | tell them kindly and clearly that you are not available | v: conformity, benevolence | identity | mark: kept your word | chance: 92
- U | keep a careful distance while you work out what is missing at home | v: security, self-direction | chance: 75
- B | flirt back, since it might do your career some good | v: power, achievement | chance: 45
- R | start an affair with them | v: hedonism, stimulation | mark: broke your word | closed: approval: betraying your partner; backfire: found out, and the relationship in crisis | chance: 40
- G | let the feeling pass, as feelings do, and stay where your roots are | v: tradition, security | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 72
- W>B | report the flirting to the office first, to protect your own position | v: security, power | closed: approval: reporting a colleague to cover yourself; backfire: a counter-complaint | chance: 45
- U>R | arrange the work trips so the two of you are alone, and nobody notices | v: hedonism, stimulation | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: deceiving your partner; backfire: found out | chance: 35
- B>G | mention the flirting at home, so your partner remembers what they have | v: security | chance: 58
- R>W | confess the crush to your partner in a rush, so nothing is hidden | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: owned up | chance: 75
- G>U | let the feeling run its course, and watch what it shows about your life | v: self-direction | chance: 65
works: The moment passes, and what is left of it is a story, or a choice made with open eyes.
works: {N} comes out of it clearer about what matters at home.
fails: People at work have started to talk.
fails: {partner} finds a message and asks what it means.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 6: calls WB (cycle s1) / calls URG (cycle s3)

== a bonus or a raise
tier: everyday
at: employer
stages: adult
age: 24-60
calls: WB
tone: joy
life: money, work
horizon: week
roles: boss, partner, parent, sibling
earth: a bonus worth a month's pay lands in your account
times: Most employees, a raise or bonus that size every few years; small yearly raises are commoner (estimate)
gap: 2-4
